First attempt at fixing the course page:

export default async function CoursePage({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const user = await getSessionUser();
  const course = await getCourse(slug);                 // ~120 ms
  const progress = await getProgress(user.id, slug);    // ~1,400 ms
  return (
    <>
      <CourseHeader course={course} />
      <Suspense fallback={<ProgressSkeleton />}>
        <ProgressPanel progress={progress} />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}

(ProgressPanel only renders the progress prop it receives; it awaits nothing.)

Statement: "Because ProgressPanel is wrapped in Suspense, the browser receives the course header after about 120 ms and the progress panel streams in later."

ModuleList, a server component outside any Suspense boundary:

async function ModuleList({ slug }: { slug: string }) {
  const modules = await getModules(slug);
  const reviews = await getReviewSummary(slug);
  return <Modules modules={modules} reviews={reviews} />;
}

Server log for one request:
+0 ms    getModules        start
+152 ms  getModules        done
+152 ms  getReviewSummary  start
+290 ms  getReviewSummary  done

getReviewSummary(slug) reads only the slug; it uses nothing returned by getModules.

Statement: "Starting both loaders together with Promise.all([getModules(slug), getReviewSummary(slug)]) should bring this component's data time to about 150 ms, the time of the slower loader."
